1|0@MensRights^1440035554#cu90igb:firstpitchthrow$:Just about every push for every law ever has been emotion-driven, not data driven. When was the last time people wrote letters to their congressmen such as the following: Dear member of congress, We really would appreciate it if the level of flouride in drinking water was reduced from 4.0 mg/L to 3.95 mg/L. Studies show that as people age, the amount of flouride they consume via drinking water increases, and at a threshold limit, poses health risks. People are living far longer today than they were in 1973 when the EPA set the current regulatory limit. It is our belief that the issue is worth re-assessing today, and I call on you, my member of congress, to take a look at the facts of this case. Many credible, peer-reviewed scientific assessments assert that a slight reduction of flourination could enhance public health, with no long term reduction in dental health. I would appreciate your prompt attention to this matter. If you would like, I can send you the scientific studies in question for review by your office. Thank you for your time and consideration, A concerned constituent. What do you think people will think of that? Right. Its boring. Its a fact based argument (even though I don't know if its true or not, that's not the point, its the structure). There's no emotional charge, no means to rally support. Contrast it with these letters: Dear buffoonish whore of the liberal media, Stop killing babies, or I won't vote for you. When you get to the afterlife, I hope you go straight to hell for funding planned parenthood. Suck on it, A concerned constituent. You can build a campaign around the emotionally charged issue of killing babies and the gates of st. peter. You can get protests and counter-protests of that. No one wants to here what the facts actually are about whether abortion is good or bad, everyone who hears stats that disagree with their opinion assumes the stats are misleading and believes the ones that support their position. Human beings feel what they believe, and gather stats to support that position, not the other way around. For another example, try this: Dear fuckhole, Stay the hell away from my guns, or I'll come for you and shoot up your whole family. Vote against the second amendment and you'll be the next Gabby Gifford. Rot in hell, a concerned citizen. See how much more attention you pay to that one? (and by attention, I mean pass it along to the FBI) The message is simple, you can build a movement around it. Neither of the last two letters I gave as examples has a shred of evidence in either one, they're both "feelzz" letters (that doesn't mean, FWIW, that I disagree with either premise, I won't say what I think about either issue and that's not relevant). Facts and scientific studies on boring subjects, presented in rational, boring ways that don't have any charge always get ignored. Your congressmen or woman votes for laws based on how their constituents feel, or how their corporate overlords feel. 
